## Step 1: Define the Node
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int data;              // data part
    struct Node *next;     // pointer to next node
};
```

👉 Here,  
- `struct Node` = blueprint for one box in the linked list.  
- `data` = the value stored.  
- `next` = address of the next box (or `NULL` if last).  

---

## Step 2: Create a new node
```c
struct Node* createNode(int value) {
    struct Node *newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = NULL;
    return newNode;
}
```

👉 Breakdown:
- `malloc(sizeof(struct Node))` → gives us memory for one node.  
- `newNode->data = value;` → put value in data part.  
- `newNode->next = NULL;` → at start, no next.  

---

## Step 3: Insert at Beginning
```c
void insertAtBeginning(struct Node **head, int value) {
    struct Node *newNode = createNode(value);
    newNode->next = *head;   // point new node to old head
    *head = newNode;         // move head to new node
}
```

👉 If head was pointing to first node, now head will point to our new node.  

---

## Step 4: Insert at End
```c
void insertAtEnd(struct Node **head, int value) {
    struct Node *newNode = createNode(value);

    if (*head == NULL) {     // list empty
        *head = newNode;
        return;
    }

    struct Node *temp = *head;
    while (temp->next != NULL) {   // go till last node
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    temp->next = newNode;   // attach at end
}
```

---

## Step 5: Print the List
```c
void printList(struct Node *head) {
    struct Node *temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        printf("%d -> ", temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    printf("NULL\n");
}
```

